las vegas has a familiar look mostly  single family homes and single-story  businesses our valley is spread out but  at city hall there's a new effort to  help the city expand by changing our  skyline john langler looks at how las  vegas could grow up  in las vegas medical district it's  already underway you can hear change  right behind us this used to be home to  small offices and small homes now it's  unlv's new medical school our community  is growing faster than we can really  keep up it's one of the most visible  examples of a metamorphosis las vegas  literally growing up city councilman  brian knudsen for so long las vegas has  been full of dirt lots  but it no longer meets the need of our  community bottom line las vegas needs  more homes knudsen wants to encourage  developers to do more with less  rethinking how empty lots like these can  be maximized connecting dense homes with  transportation and services if we go up  to three four five six seven stories  you start to see a lot of more resources  for our community and a lot more  opportunities some parts of the plan  have already been approved by the  council more are on the way i think the  biggest challenge we have is imagination  you can't stop change jessica saylor can  imagine taller neighbors she lives in  scotch 80s the medical district is a  taller neighbor she can already see i'm  hoping that we can find a balance  between what it is that needs to be done  to support the area and the demands that  the city has years ago she expressed  concerns about older neighborhoods being  overwhelmed by new development  now she has a more pragmatic approach  you can't fight against it but i think  you can work with it key to the plan are  incentives density bonuses making it  more appealing for companies to build  taller buildings likely it would involve  discounts on permits and other tedious  parts of construction that can cost  millions i think it scares people and i  understand it thinking about a big  looming building is not what i thought  about when i thought about las vegas but  it's what knudsen hopes becomes the  city's future i think this is the  beginning of a very different las vegas  john langler live local now
 

